window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"6e452456156b9a73d11f2e66e03e7380acb39cc4628b6567bb91b91b05853f0c":{"markdown":"## Exact scope\n\nSB-1–SB-4 and all six numbered identities are retained. Every scalar input now has a written earlier CF, SC, SF or FF proof; no historical contour, compact-trace or product-measure provider is a premise. This closes the bounded analytic input route for the general Hilbert-algebra argument. It does not prove the separate arbitrary-weight GNS and Hilbert-algebra correspondence.","html":"<h2>Exact scope<\/h2>\n<p>SB-1–SB-4 and all six numbered identities are retained. Every scalar input now has a written earlier CF, SC, SF or FF proof; no historical contour, compact-trace or product-measure provider is a premise. This closes the bounded analytic input route for the general Hilbert-algebra argument. It does not prove the separate arbitrary-weight GNS and Hilbert-algebra correspondence.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MF-SB.md","start_offset":12962,"end_offset":13356,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6ebf8098f36556a1e6a58e99e5cd4e3ec95de7d541a54a4e758c210fd7c0bc0c":{"markdown":"## 1. The theorem and its normalization\n\nLet \\(M\\) be a concrete von Neumann algebra on a complex Hilbert space, and let \\(h\\), \\(k\\) be positive injective self-adjoint operators affiliated with \\(M\\). No separability, semifiniteness or bounded inverse is assumed. For \\(a>0\\) let\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nS_a=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:-a\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq0\\},\n\\qquad u_t=h^{it}k^{-it}.\n\\tag{PS1}\n\\]\n\nThe following conditions are equivalent:\n\n1. \\(u\\) extends to a contractive \\(M\\)-valued function \\(U\\) on \\(S_a\\), holomorphic in the interior and jointly intrinsically \\(\\sigma\\text{-strong}\\) continuous on the closed strip.\n2. \\(D(k^a)\\) is contained in \\(D(h^a)\\), and \\(\\|h^av\\|\\leq\\|k^av\\|\\) for every \\(v\\) in \\(D(k^a)\\).\n\nCondition 2 is precisely \\(h^{2a}\\leq k^{2a}\\) as closed positive forms. The extension is unique, norm-holomorphic in the interior and jointly intrinsically \\(\\sigma\\text{-strong-*}\\) continuous on the closed strip. If \\(T\\) denotes the unique bounded contraction extending \\(h^ak^{-a}\\) on its full natural domain \\(D(k^{-a})\\), then\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps2\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nU(t-ia)=h^{it}T k^{-it}.\n\\tag{PS2}\n\\]\n\nPut \\(H=h^a,\\quad K=k^a\\). The width-one product \\(H^{it}K^{-it}=u_{at}\\) has extension \\(V(w)=U(aw)\\), and \\(U(z)=V(z/a)\\). It therefore suffices to prove the result for the full domain inequality \\(D(K)\\subseteq D(H),\\quad\\|Hv\\|\\leq\\|Kv\\|\\), and \\(S_1\\). All inner products below are linear in the first variable.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l158.endpoint\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"OA-FLOW.XII5.STRIP.ENDPOINT\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"oa-flow.xii5.strip.endpoint\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>1. The theorem and its normalization<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> be a concrete von Neumann algebra on a complex Hilbert space, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> be positive injective self-adjoint operators affiliated with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. No separability, semifiniteness or bounded inverse is assumed. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a&gt;0\\)\">\\(a&gt;0\\)<\/span> let<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nS_a=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:-a\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq0\\},\n\\qquad u_t=h^{it}k^{-it}.\n\\tag{PS1}\n\\]\">\\[\nS_a=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:-a\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq0\\},\n\\qquad u_t=h^{it}k^{-it}.\n\\tag{PS1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The following conditions are equivalent:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> extends to a contractive <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>-valued function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_a\\)\">\\(S_a\\)<\/span>, holomorphic in the interior and jointly intrinsically <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\text{-strong}\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\text{-strong}\\)<\/span> continuous on the closed strip.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(k^a)\\)\">\\(D(k^a)\\)<\/span> is contained in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(h^a)\\)\">\\(D(h^a)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h^av\\|\\leq\\|k^av\\|\\)\">\\(\\|h^av\\|\\leq\\|k^av\\|\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(k^a)\\)\">\\(D(k^a)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Condition 2 is precisely <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h^{2a}\\leq k^{2a}\\)\">\\(h^{2a}\\leq k^{2a}\\)<\/span> as closed positive forms. The extension is unique, norm-holomorphic in the interior and jointly intrinsically <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\text{-strong-*}\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\text{-strong-*}\\)<\/span> continuous on the closed strip. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> denotes the unique bounded contraction extending <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h^ak^{-a}\\)\">\\(h^ak^{-a}\\)<\/span> on its full natural domain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(k^{-a})\\)\">\\(D(k^{-a})\\)<\/span>, then<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nU(t-ia)=h^{it}T k^{-it}.\n\\tag{PS2}\n\\]\">\\[\nU(t-ia)=h^{it}T k^{-it}.\n\\tag{PS2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=h^a,\\quad K=k^a\\)\">\\(H=h^a,\\quad K=k^a\\)<\/span>. The width-one product <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H^{it}K^{-it}=u_{at}\\)\">\\(H^{it}K^{-it}=u_{at}\\)<\/span> has extension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(w)=U(aw)\\)\">\\(V(w)=U(aw)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U(z)=V(z/a)\\)\">\\(U(z)=V(z/a)\\)<\/span>. It therefore suffices to prove the result for the full domain inequality <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(K)\\subseteq D(H),\\quad\\|Hv\\|\\leq\\|Kv\\|\\)\">\\(D(K)\\subseteq D(H),\\quad\\|Hv\\|\\leq\\|Kv\\|\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_1\\)\">\\(S_1\\)<\/span>. All inner products below are linear in the first variable.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L158.md","start_offset":1015,"end_offset":2593,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6e121071a4aab25df514427dbf5af994193fe65323e81342ef56719042a936e4":{"markdown":"<a id=\"gda-10\"><\/a>\n\n## GDA10. A scalar composition determines the operator-valued map\n\nThe extended-value and form arguments here use [EP2–5](OA-FLOW-EP.md#ep-2), the dense GNS left module of [GW1–4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1), its faithful normal representation in [NF5](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.5), and [SB4's unbounded spectral domains and graph cutoffs](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb4), together with [SF1's affiliation and normal transport](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf1). A direct uniqueness argument in the operator-valued setting is also given in [compatible modular operator-valued weights](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-OE.html#faithfulness-semifiniteness-and-direct-uniqueness).\n\n**Theorem.** Let \\(P\\subseteq N\\) be von Neumann algebras and let \\(S_1,S_2:N_+\\to\\widehat P_+\\) be operator-valued weights. If a faithful normal semifinite weight \\(\\varphi\\) on \\(P\\) satisfies\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdau1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\overline\\varphi\\circ S_1=\\overline\\varphi\\circ S_2\n \\quad\\hbox{on all }N_+,\n \\tag{GDAU1}\n\\]\n\nthen \\(S_1=S_2\\). The bar denotes EP5's evaluation on the entire extended positive cone. Consequently the map \\(T\\) of GDA1–8 is the unique operator-valued weight with its scalar composition identities for all faithful normal semifinite input weights. No countability assumption is made.\n\n**Proof.** Denote the common scalar composition by \\(\\Phi\\), fix \\(X\\in N_+\\), and set \\(Y_i=S_i(X)\\). In the faithful normal GNS representation of \\(P\\), write \\(D=\\Lambda_\\varphi(\\mathfrak n_\\varphi)\\). GW proves that \\(D\\) is dense and invariant under every element of \\(P\\). Let \\(q_i\\) be the extended closed quadratic form of \\(Y_i\\), including the component where its value is infinite. For \\(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\), bounded spectral truncations, the GNS identity and EP4–5 give\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdau2\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n q_i(\\Lambda_\\varphi(a))\n =\\overline\\varphi(a^*Y_i a)\n =\\Phi(a^*Xa).\n \\tag{GDAU2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed for bounded positive \\(b\\in P\\) the first identity is \\(\\langle\\pi_\\varphi(b)\\Lambda_\\varphi(a),\\Lambda_\\varphi(a)\\rangle=\\varphi(a^*ba)\\). The normal vector functional and normal congruence extend it to \\(Y_i\\); the second identity is \\(P\\)-bimodularity of \\(S_i\\). Thus \\(q_1=q_2\\) on \\(D\\), with infinite values retained.\n\nTo pass from this equality to the entire forms, write \\((e_i,A_i)\\) for EP's finite-part projection and positive affiliated operator representing \\(Y_i\\). Let \\(e_{i,n}=1_{[0,n]}(A_i)\\), zero on the infinite component. Its range satisfies \\(q_i\\leq n\\|\\cdot\\|^2\\), and \\(e_{i,n}D\\subseteq D\\). Spectral truncation approximates each finite-domain vector \\(\\xi\\) by \\(e_{i,n}\\xi\\) in the form norm \\(\\|\\xi\\|_{q_i}=(\\|\\xi\\|^2+q_i(\\xi))^{1/2}\\). For fixed \\(n\\), choose \\(d_j\\in D\\) with \\(d_j\\to\\xi\\) in Hilbert norm. Then\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdau3\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\|e_{i,n}(d_j-\\xi)\\|_{q_i}\n \\leq\\sqrt{1+n}\\,\\|d_j-\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{GDAU3}\n\\]\n\nIt follows that \\(D\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}(q_i)\\) is a form core, and its Hilbert closure is the finite-part space \\(e_iH_\\varphi\\). Equation(GDAU2) makes the two finite subsets of \\(D\\) equal and their polarized forms equal. Closing the common core gives identical entire closed forms and identical finite-part projections. EP3's uniqueness therefore gives \\(Y_1=Y_2\\). Since \\(X\\) was arbitrary, \\(S_1=S_2\\). No subtraction of infinite weight values, or continuity of an unbounded weight on a merely dense positive set, is used.\n\nFor the canonical-map conclusion one faithful normal semifinite scalar input is sufficient. Such an input exists on every nonzero concrete \\(P\\subseteq B(K)\\), without a faithful-state assumption. For a nonzero projection \\(p\\), choose nonzero \\(v\\in pK\\) and let \\(e\\) project onto \\(\\overline{P'v}\\). The subspace reduces \\(P'\\), so \\(e\\in P\\) and \\(e\\leq p\\), by [CF8's projection construction](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.8) and [BD1's bicommutant characterization](OA-FLOW-BD.md#oa-flow.bd.1). The normalized vector functional is faithful on \\(ePe\\): if \\(a\\in(ePe)_+\\) has zero value, \\(a^{1/2}\\) kills \\(v\\), hence kills \\(P'v\\) by commutation, and therefore \\(a=0\\).\n\nChoose a maximal mutually orthogonal family of these nonzero projections \\(e_i\\), with unit vectors \\(v_i\\). Their join is one: a nonzero complementary projection would supply a further pair by the same construction. Define\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdau4\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\varphi(a)=\\sup_{F\\subseteq I,\\ F\\text{ finite}}\n       \\sum_{i\\in F}\\langle av_i,v_i\\rangle\n \\quad(a\\in P_+).\n \\tag{GDAU4}\n\\]\n\nA common finite superset of indices proves additivity and homogeneity. Vector functionals are normal by [CP4/6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.4) and the bounded increasing-operator lemma [WF2](OA-FLOW-WF.md#oa-flow.wf.2). Interchanging the two nonnegative suprema for a bounded increasing positive net proves normality of(GDAU4). If its value is zero, \\(a^{1/2}\\) kills every \\(v_i\\) and \\(P'v_i\\), hence all \\(K\\), so it is faithful. For finite \\(F\\), \\(p_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}e_i\\) has \\(\\varphi(p_F)=|F|<\\infty\\). These projections increase strongly to one; [GW4's finite-projection criterion](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.4) proves semifiniteness. The maximal principle is the earlier CF1 choice convention, and the index set may be arbitrary. Applying(GDAU1) to this one input proves uniqueness of \\(T\\). On the zero algebra the only map is zero. \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>GDA10. A scalar composition determines the operator-valued map<\/h2>\n<p>The extended-value and form arguments here use <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EP.html#ep-2\">EP2–5<\/a>, the dense GNS left module of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW1–4<\/a>, its faithful normal representation in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.5\">NF5<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb4\">SB4's unbounded spectral domains and graph cutoffs<\/a>, together with <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf1\">SF1's affiliation and normal transport<\/a>. A direct uniqueness argument in the operator-valued setting is also given in <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-OE.html#faithfulness-semifiniteness-and-direct-uniqueness\">compatible modular operator-valued weights<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\subseteq N\\)\">\\(P\\subseteq N\\)<\/span> be von Neumann algebras and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_1,S_2:N_+\\to\\widehat P_+\\)\">\\(S_1,S_2:N_+\\to\\widehat P_+\\)<\/span> be operator-valued weights. If a faithful normal semifinite weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> satisfies<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\overline\\varphi\\circ S_1=\\overline\\varphi\\circ S_2\n \\quad\\hbox{on all }N_+,\n \\tag{GDAU1}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\overline\\varphi\\circ S_1=\\overline\\varphi\\circ S_2\n \\quad\\hbox{on all }N_+,\n \\tag{GDAU1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_1=S_2\\)\">\\(S_1=S_2\\)<\/span>. The bar denotes EP5's evaluation on the entire extended positive cone. Consequently the map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> of GDA1–8 is the unique operator-valued weight with its scalar composition identities for all faithful normal semifinite input weights. No countability assumption is made.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Denote the common scalar composition by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Phi\\)<\/span>, fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\in N_+\\)\">\\(X\\in N_+\\)<\/span>, and set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_i=S_i(X)\\)\">\\(Y_i=S_i(X)\\)<\/span>. In the faithful normal GNS representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D=\\Lambda_\\varphi(\\mathfrak n_\\varphi)\\)\">\\(D=\\Lambda_\\varphi(\\mathfrak n_\\varphi)\\)<\/span>. GW proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is dense and invariant under every element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_i\\)\">\\(q_i\\)<\/span> be the extended closed quadratic form of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_i\\)\">\\(Y_i\\)<\/span>, including the component where its value is infinite. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\)\">\\(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, bounded spectral truncations, the GNS identity and EP4–5 give<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q_i(\\Lambda_\\varphi(a))\n =\\overline\\varphi(a^*Y_i a)\n =\\Phi(a^*Xa).\n \\tag{GDAU2}\n\\]\">\\[\n q_i(\\Lambda_\\varphi(a))\n =\\overline\\varphi(a^*Y_i a)\n =\\Phi(a^*Xa).\n \\tag{GDAU2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Indeed for bounded positive <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in P\\)\">\\(b\\in P\\)<\/span> the first identity is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle\\pi_\\varphi(b)\\Lambda_\\varphi(a),\\Lambda_\\varphi(a)\\rangle=\\varphi(a^*ba)\\)\">\\(\\langle\\pi_\\varphi(b)\\Lambda_\\varphi(a),\\Lambda_\\varphi(a)\\rangle=\\varphi(a^*ba)\\)<\/span>. The normal vector functional and normal congruence extend it to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_i\\)\">\\(Y_i\\)<\/span>; the second identity is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>-bimodularity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_i\\)\">\\(S_i\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_1=q_2\\)\">\\(q_1=q_2\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, with infinite values retained.<\/p>\n<p>To pass from this equality to the entire forms, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_i,A_i)\\)\">\\((e_i,A_i)\\)<\/span> for EP's finite-part projection and positive affiliated operator representing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_i\\)\">\\(Y_i\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_{i,n}=1_{[0,n]}(A_i)\\)\">\\(e_{i,n}=1_{[0,n]}(A_i)\\)<\/span>, zero on the infinite component. Its range satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_i\\leq n\\|\\cdot\\|^2\\)\">\\(q_i\\leq n\\|\\cdot\\|^2\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_{i,n}D\\subseteq D\\)\">\\(e_{i,n}D\\subseteq D\\)<\/span>. Spectral truncation approximates each finite-domain vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_{i,n}\\xi\\)\">\\(e_{i,n}\\xi\\)<\/span> in the form norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\xi\\|_{q_i}=(\\|\\xi\\|^2+q_i(\\xi))^{1/2}\\)\">\\(\\|\\xi\\|_{q_i}=(\\|\\xi\\|^2+q_i(\\xi))^{1/2}\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j\\in D\\)\">\\(d_j\\in D\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j\\to\\xi\\)\">\\(d_j\\to\\xi\\)<\/span> in Hilbert norm. Then<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|e_{i,n}(d_j-\\xi)\\|_{q_i}\n \\leq\\sqrt{1+n}\\,\\|d_j-\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{GDAU3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|e_{i,n}(d_j-\\xi)\\|_{q_i}\n \\leq\\sqrt{1+n}\\,\\|d_j-\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{GDAU3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>It follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}(q_i)\\)\">\\(D\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}(q_i)\\)<\/span> is a form core, and its Hilbert closure is the finite-part space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_iH_\\varphi\\)\">\\(e_iH_\\varphi\\)<\/span>. Equation(GDAU2) makes the two finite subsets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> equal and their polarized forms equal. Closing the common core gives identical entire closed forms and identical finite-part projections. EP3's uniqueness therefore gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_1=Y_2\\)\">\\(Y_1=Y_2\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> was arbitrary, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_1=S_2\\)\">\\(S_1=S_2\\)<\/span>. No subtraction of infinite weight values, or continuity of an unbounded weight on a merely dense positive set, is used.<\/p>\n<p>For the canonical-map conclusion one faithful normal semifinite scalar input is sufficient. Such an input exists on every nonzero concrete <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\subseteq B(K)\\)\">\\(P\\subseteq B(K)\\)<\/span>, without a faithful-state assumption. For a nonzero projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, choose nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in pK\\)\">\\(v\\in pK\\)<\/span> and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> project onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline{P&#x27;v}\\)\">\\(\\overline{P&#x27;v}\\)<\/span>. The subspace reduces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P&#x27;\\)\">\\(P&#x27;\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\in P\\)\">\\(e\\in P\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\leq p\\)\">\\(e\\leq p\\)<\/span>, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.8\">CF8's projection construction<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BD.html#oa-flow.bd.1\">BD1's bicommutant characterization<\/a>. The normalized vector functional is faithful on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ePe\\)\">\\(ePe\\)<\/span>: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in(ePe)_+\\)\">\\(a\\in(ePe)_+\\)<\/span> has zero value, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^{1/2}\\)\">\\(a^{1/2}\\)<\/span> kills <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span>, hence kills <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P&#x27;v\\)\">\\(P&#x27;v\\)<\/span> by commutation, and therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=0\\)\">\\(a=0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a maximal mutually orthogonal family of these nonzero projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_i\\)\">\\(e_i\\)<\/span>, with unit vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_i\\)\">\\(v_i\\)<\/span>. Their join is one: a nonzero complementary projection would supply a further pair by the same construction. Define<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\varphi(a)=\\sup_{F\\subseteq I,\\ F\\text{ finite}}\n       \\sum_{i\\in F}\\langle av_i,v_i\\rangle\n \\quad(a\\in P_+).\n \\tag{GDAU4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\varphi(a)=\\sup_{F\\subseteq I,\\ F\\text{ finite}}\n       \\sum_{i\\in F}\\langle av_i,v_i\\rangle\n \\quad(a\\in P_+).\n \\tag{GDAU4}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>A common finite superset of indices proves additivity and homogeneity. Vector functionals are normal by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.4\">CP4/6<\/a> and the bounded increasing-operator lemma <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WF.html#oa-flow.wf.2\">WF2<\/a>. Interchanging the two nonnegative suprema for a bounded increasing positive net proves normality of(GDAU4). If its value is zero, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^{1/2}\\)\">\\(a^{1/2}\\)<\/span> kills every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_i\\)\">\\(v_i\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P&#x27;v_i\\)\">\\(P&#x27;v_i\\)<\/span>, hence all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, so it is faithful. For finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}e_i\\)\">\\(p_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}e_i\\)<\/span> has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(p_F)=|F|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\varphi(p_F)=|F|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. These projections increase strongly to one; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.4\">GW4's finite-projection criterion<\/a> proves semifiniteness. The maximal principle is the earlier CF1 choice convention, and the index set may be arbitrary. Applying(GDAU1) to this one input proves uniqueness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>. On the zero algebra the only map is zero. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-GDA.md","start_offset":39468,"end_offset":44783,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6ea00db566dc919c3b8ca3f64fb3ded6270ac2aa884183227805acfa17b24974":{"markdown":"## IS8. Exercises with solutions\n\n**Exercise1.** Replace \\(r^{-1/2}\\) in (IS38)'s \\(J\\) by \\(1\\). What fails?\n\n**Solution.** The product measure is \\(db\\,dr/r\\), while ambient Haar is \\(db\\,dr/r^2\\). The unweighted map has squared norm \\(\\int|\\xi(b,r)|^2db\\,dr/r\\), rather than the Haar squared norm. It is not isometric. Its conjugated right translation also retains the incorrect scalar \\(c^{-1/2}\\); the correct \\(H\\) action has \\(\\Delta_H=1\\).\n\n**Exercise2.** Why can IS6 use an uncountable basis without intersecting an uncountable family of conull sets?\n\n**Solution.** The slices are bounded operators rather than pointwise field representatives. Each finite matrix compression belongs to the algebra, and the net of all finite compressions converges strongly. No exceptional set is selected or intersected.\n\n**Exercise3.** Does (IS7) prove that every system with a central quotient embedding is induced?\n\n**Solution.** No. It is a forward property of the already defined fixed algebra (IS3). Recovering fibers from an arbitrary embedding requires a separate recognition theorem with exact measurable generation and representation-independence hypotheses. That converse is not used in (IS28)–(IS34).\n\nFurther reading: M. Takesaki, [*Theory of Operator Algebras II*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4), Definition X.4.10 and Theorem X.4.12, pp.301 and 303–305.\n\n<a id=\"is-figure\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>IS8. Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise1.<\/strong> Replace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{-1/2}\\)\">\\(r^{-1/2}\\)<\/span> in (IS38)'s <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. What fails?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The product measure is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(db\\,dr/r\\)\">\\(db\\,dr/r\\)<\/span>, while ambient Haar is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(db\\,dr/r^2\\)\">\\(db\\,dr/r^2\\)<\/span>. The unweighted map has squared norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int|\\xi(b,r)|^2db\\,dr/r\\)\">\\(\\int|\\xi(b,r)|^2db\\,dr/r\\)<\/span>, rather than the Haar squared norm. It is not isometric. Its conjugated right translation also retains the incorrect scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c^{-1/2}\\)\">\\(c^{-1/2}\\)<\/span>; the correct <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> action has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta_H=1\\)\">\\(\\Delta_H=1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise2.<\/strong> Why can IS6 use an uncountable basis without intersecting an uncountable family of conull sets?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The slices are bounded operators rather than pointwise field representatives. Each finite matrix compression belongs to the algebra, and the net of all finite compressions converges strongly. No exceptional set is selected or intersected.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise3.<\/strong> Does (IS7) prove that every system with a central quotient embedding is induced?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> No. It is a forward property of the already defined fixed algebra (IS3). Recovering fibers from an arbitrary embedding requires a separate recognition theorem with exact measurable generation and representation-independence hypotheses. That converse is not used in (IS28)–(IS34).<\/p>\n<p>Further reading: M. Takesaki, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em><\/a>, Definition X.4.10 and Theorem X.4.12, pp.301 and 303–305.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-IS.md","start_offset":38313,"end_offset":39710,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6ea5c9d9a09e3810fe314069c7903e5116b18d59880f5718bc4003c34f38edbc":{"markdown":"<a id=\"lft-4\"><\/a>\n## 4. An onto Fourier realization of the core\n\nForm the regular core \\(C_\\varphi=M\\rtimes_{\\sigma^\\varphi}\\mathbb R\\) on\n\\(\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\), using Lebesgue measure \\(dr\\). To distinguish its coefficient embedding from the original diagonal \\(\\pi\\), write\n\\[\n (\\Pi_\\varphi(A)\\xi)(r)=\\sigma_{-r}^\\varphi(A)\\xi(r),\n \\qquad (\\Lambda(t)\\xi)(r)=\\xi(r-t).\n\\tag{LFT4.a}\n\\]\nThe [normal regular representation](OA-FLOW-NR.md#oa-flow.nr.3) makes these a faithful normal copy of \\(M\\) and its covariant translation group. Their generated algebra is \\(C_\\varphi\\). Its dual action \\(\\widehat\\theta_s\\) fixes \\(\\Pi_\\varphi(M)\\) and sends \\(\\Lambda(t)\\) to \\(e^{-ist}\\Lambda(t)\\); on \\(\\mathcal K\\) it is implemented by multiplication by \\(e^{-isr}\\), as proved in [DA's spatial dual action](OA-FLOW-DA.md#da-action).\n\nUse the negative Fourier transform in \\(r\\):\n\\[\n \\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\n           \\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-irp}\\xi(y,x;r)\\,dr.\n\\tag{LFT4.b}\n\\]\nThe integral formula is initially for integrable square-integrable tensors. The [onto scalar Plancherel theorem](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.3), followed by completion of finite Hilbert tensors, defines the full unitary on \\(\\mathcal K\\). Thus (LFT4.b) never requires an ordinary convergent integral for an arbitrary \\(L^2\\) vector.\n\nRecall from Section 1 that the lifted point over \\(y\\in Gx\\), for base point \\((x,\\lambda)\\), has height\n\\(\\lambda_y=\\delta(x,y)\\lambda\\). Define\n\\[\n (W\\xi)(y,x;\\lambda)\n    =\\lambda^{-1/2}\\widehat\\xi\n       \\bigl(y,x;\\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)\\bigr).\n\\tag{LFT4.c}\n\\]\nThis is a unitary from the entire regular Hilbert space \\(\\mathcal K\\) onto\n\\(\\widetilde H=L^2(E\\times L,\\nu_r\\,d\\lambda)\\). Indeed the substitution\n\\(p=\\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)\\) gives \\(dp=d\\lambda/\\lambda\\), so nonnegative scalar interchange and Plancherel give\n\\[\n \\int_E\\int_0^\\infty |W\\xi(y,x;\\lambda)|^2\\,d\\lambda\\,d\\nu_r\n       =\\int_E\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)|^2\\,dp\\,d\\nu_r\n       =\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\tag{LFT4.d}\n\\]\nAll functions in this change of variables are Borel, and for each \\((y,x)\\) the substitution preserves the relevant null classes. Tonelli therefore makes the formula independent of the chosen measurable representative. There is a full inverse after Fourier transformation:\n\\[\n \\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)\n   =\\bigl(\\delta(y,x)e^p\\bigr)^{1/2}\n       \\eta\\bigl(y,x;\\delta(y,x)e^p\\bigr),\n       \\qquad\\eta\\in\\widetilde H.\n\\tag{LFT4.e}\n\\]\nThe same substitution proves that the right side is square integrable with norm \\(\\|\\eta\\|\\). Apply the inverse Fourier unitary to obtain \\(\\xi\\). This establishes surjectivity, with no unspecified completion or multiplicity space.\n\n### Compute on one orbit arrow, then on the whole algebra\n\nLet \\(\\widetilde\\pi(F)\\) and \\(\\widetilde u_g\\) be the lifted generators of \\(N\\), and let \\(j:M\\to N\\) be the lambda-independent field embedding from Section 3. The [modular matrix formula](OA-FLOW-REL.md#rel-5) gives\n\\[\n (\\Pi_\\varphi(u_g)\\xi)(y,x;r)\n     =\\delta(y,g^{-1}y)^{-ir}\\xi(g^{-1}y,x;r).\n\\tag{LFT4.f}\n\\]\nPut \\(z=g^{-1}y\\). Negative Fourier transformation turns the scalar multiplier in (LFT4.f) into the shift\n\\(p\\mapsto p+\\log\\delta(y,z)\\). But the cocycle identity gives\n\\[\n \\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)+\\log\\delta(y,z)\n        =\\log(\\delta(x,z)\\lambda).\n\\tag{LFT4.g}\n\\]\nThe factor \\(\\lambda^{-1/2}\\) in \\(W\\) is unchanged. Diagonal multipliers have no Fourier shift, since their original modular action is trivial. Consequently\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(\\pi(f))W^*&=\\widetilde\\pi(f(y))=j(\\pi(f)),\\\\\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(u_g)W^*&=\\widetilde u_g=j(u_g),\\\\\n W\\Lambda(t)W^*&=\\widetilde\\pi(\\lambda_y^{-it}).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\tag{LFT4.h}\n\\]\nThe notation in the last line means multiplication by \\(\\lambda_y^{-it}\\) on the represented lifted relation; its function on the lifted base is \\((y,a)\\mapsto a^{-it}\\). The last equality follows because the Fourier transform of \\(\\xi(r-t)\\) is \\(e^{-itp}\\widehat\\xi(p)\\).\n\nThe translation and modulation identities hold on all of \\(L^2\\), by the scalar theorem and tensor completion. Formula (LFT4.g) is a pointwise identity on the invariant conull relation. These facts justify (LFT4.h) as identities of bounded operators on their full Hilbert spaces. Moreover both maps\n\\(A\\mapsto W\\Pi_\\varphi(A)W^*\\) and \\(A\\mapsto j(A)\\) are normal. They agree on the unital star algebra spanned by \\(\\pi(f)u_g\\), so ultraweak density and normality yield\n\\[\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(A)W^*=j(A)\\qquad(A\\in M).\n\\tag{LFT4.i}\n\\]\nNo Fourier integral has been interchanged with an arbitrary infinite orbit-matrix sum.\n\nWe also need the entire image algebra. The functions \\(a\\mapsto a^{-it}\\) generate every bounded Borel function of \\(a\\in L\\). One explicit verification uses only \\(t=1/n\\): the continuous map\n\\[\n r\\longmapsto(e^{-ir/n})_{n\\ge1}\\quad\n     \\text{from }\\mathbb R\\text{ to }\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N}\n\\tag{LFT4.j}\n\\]\nis injective, since a nonzero difference cannot be an integer multiple of \\(2\\pi n\\) for every sufficiently large \\(n\\). Its image is Borel and its inverse on that image is Borel by the [Borel image and inverse theorem](../../NCG-FOLIATIONS/companions/polish-spaces-and-standard-borel-spaces.html#oa-fnd-pb-06). Spectral projections of the real and imaginary coordinate multipliers belong to their generated von Neumann algebra by the [bounded Borel spectral calculus](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf1.spectral-calculus). The class of sets whose characteristic multipliers belong is closed under complements and countable disjoint unions, using bounded strong limits. A monotone-class argument therefore gives every Borel function of \\(r\\), and hence every bounded Borel function of \\(a=e^r\\).\n\nTogether with \\(\\widetilde\\pi(f(y))\\), these multipliers give every \\(\\widetilde\\pi(F)\\) for \\(F\\in L^\\infty(X\\times L,\\mu\\,d\\lambda)\\). To see the joint assertion, rectangle indicators belong; their finite intersections are again rectangles. Complements and disjoint bounded strong sums give a lambda-system, so the [proved pi–lambda argument](OA-FLOW-L75.md#oa-flow.kernel.integration) gives all product Borel indicators. Simple approximation gives bounded functions, and completion only changes null representatives. The representation \\(\\widetilde\\pi\\) is normal and faithful by REL2 applied to the lifted system. Thus (LFT4.h) contains all lifted diagonal generators and all \\(\\widetilde u_g\\). The reverse inclusion of generating algebras is already in (LFT4.h)–(LFT4.i), proving the spatial normal isomorphism\n\\[\n \\Gamma:C_\\varphi\\longrightarrow N,\\qquad\n \\Gamma(B)=WBW^*,\\qquad \\Gamma(C_\\varphi)=N.\n\\tag{LFT4.k}\n\\]\nIts inverse is conjugation by \\(W^*\\), also normal.\n\n### The dual flow and the full density domain\n\nFourier modulation \\(e^{-isr}\\) shifts \\(p\\) to \\(p+s\\). In (LFT4.c) this replaces \\(\\lambda\\) by \\(e^s\\lambda\\). Its square-root factor gives\n\\[\n W(e^{-isr}\\xi)(y,x;\\lambda)\n    =e^{s/2}(W\\xi)(y,x;e^s\\lambda).\n\\tag{LFT4.l}\n\\]\nThe right side is the dilation unitary implementing \\(\\theta_s\\) in Section 3. Therefore\n\\(\\Gamma\\widehat\\theta_s=\\theta_s\\Gamma\\) on the whole algebra. The core's positive nonsingular generator \\(h_\\varphi\\), characterized by\n\\(h_\\varphi^{it}=\\Lambda(t)\\), is constructed with its full spectral domains in [CORE1](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-1). The last line of (LFT4.h), or uniqueness of that self-adjoint generator, gives\n\\[\n \\Gamma(h_\\varphi)=M_{\\lambda_y^{-1}},\\qquad\n D\\bigl(\\Gamma(h_\\varphi)^z\\bigr)\n   =\\left\\{\\eta\\in\\widetilde H:\n       \\int_{E\\times L}\\lambda_y^{-2\\operatorname{Re}z}\n                    |\\eta|^2\\,d\\nu_r\\,d\\lambda<\\infty\\right\\}.\n\\tag{LFT4.m}\n\\]\nHere \\(z\\in\\mathbb C\\), and the operator value is multiplication by \\(\\lambda_y^{-z}\\) on this domain. In particular \\(\\theta_s(h_\\varphi)=e^{-s}h_\\varphi\\) in these coordinates. The reciprocal height, not the height, is forced by the chosen translation and Fourier signs.","html":"<h2>4. An onto Fourier realization of the core<\/h2>\n<p>Form the regular core <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_\\varphi=M\\rtimes_{\\sigma^\\varphi}\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(C_\\varphi=M\\rtimes_{\\sigma^\\varphi}\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> on\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)<\/span>, using Lebesgue measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dr\\)\">\\(dr\\)<\/span>. To distinguish its coefficient embedding from the original diagonal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span>, write\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (\\Pi_\\varphi(A)\\xi)(r)=\\sigma_{-r}^\\varphi(A)\\xi(r),\n \\qquad (\\Lambda(t)\\xi)(r)=\\xi(r-t).\n\\tag{LFT4.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n (\\Pi_\\varphi(A)\\xi)(r)=\\sigma_{-r}^\\varphi(A)\\xi(r),\n \\qquad (\\Lambda(t)\\xi)(r)=\\xi(r-t).\n\\tag{LFT4.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NR.html#oa-flow.nr.3\">normal regular representation<\/a> makes these a faithful normal copy of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and its covariant translation group. Their generated algebra is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_\\varphi\\)\">\\(C_\\varphi\\)<\/span>. Its dual action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat\\theta_s\\)\">\\(\\widehat\\theta_s\\)<\/span> fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi_\\varphi(M)\\)\">\\(\\Pi_\\varphi(M)\\)<\/span> and sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda(t)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda(t)\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-ist}\\Lambda(t)\\)\">\\(e^{-ist}\\Lambda(t)\\)<\/span>; on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K\\)<\/span> it is implemented by multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-isr}\\)\">\\(e^{-isr}\\)<\/span>, as proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DA.html#da-action\">DA's spatial dual action<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>Use the negative Fourier transform in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\n           \\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-irp}\\xi(y,x;r)\\,dr.\n\\tag{LFT4.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\n           \\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-irp}\\xi(y,x;r)\\,dr.\n\\tag{LFT4.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe integral formula is initially for integrable square-integrable tensors. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.3\">onto scalar Plancherel theorem<\/a>, followed by completion of finite Hilbert tensors, defines the full unitary on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K\\)<\/span>. Thus (LFT4.b) never requires an ordinary convergent integral for an arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> vector.<\/p>\n<p>Recall from Section 1 that the lifted point over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in Gx\\)\">\\(y\\in Gx\\)<\/span>, for base point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((x,\\lambda)\\)\">\\((x,\\lambda)\\)<\/span>, has height\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_y=\\delta(x,y)\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda_y=\\delta(x,y)\\lambda\\)<\/span>. Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (W\\xi)(y,x;\\lambda)\n    =\\lambda^{-1/2}\\widehat\\xi\n       \\bigl(y,x;\\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)\\bigr).\n\\tag{LFT4.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n (W\\xi)(y,x;\\lambda)\n    =\\lambda^{-1/2}\\widehat\\xi\n       \\bigl(y,x;\\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)\\bigr).\n\\tag{LFT4.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a unitary from the entire regular Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K\\)<\/span> onto\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde H=L^2(E\\times L,\\nu_r\\,d\\lambda)\\)\">\\(\\widetilde H=L^2(E\\times L,\\nu_r\\,d\\lambda)\\)<\/span>. Indeed the substitution\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=\\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)\\)\">\\(p=\\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dp=d\\lambda/\\lambda\\)\">\\(dp=d\\lambda/\\lambda\\)<\/span>, so nonnegative scalar interchange and Plancherel give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_E\\int_0^\\infty |W\\xi(y,x;\\lambda)|^2\\,d\\lambda\\,d\\nu_r\n       =\\int_E\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)|^2\\,dp\\,d\\nu_r\n       =\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\tag{LFT4.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_E\\int_0^\\infty |W\\xi(y,x;\\lambda)|^2\\,d\\lambda\\,d\\nu_r\n       =\\int_E\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)|^2\\,dp\\,d\\nu_r\n       =\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\tag{LFT4.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nAll functions in this change of variables are Borel, and for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((y,x)\\)\">\\((y,x)\\)<\/span> the substitution preserves the relevant null classes. Tonelli therefore makes the formula independent of the chosen measurable representative. There is a full inverse after Fourier transformation:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)\n   =\\bigl(\\delta(y,x)e^p\\bigr)^{1/2}\n       \\eta\\bigl(y,x;\\delta(y,x)e^p\\bigr),\n       \\qquad\\eta\\in\\widetilde H.\n\\tag{LFT4.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat\\xi(y,x;p)\n   =\\bigl(\\delta(y,x)e^p\\bigr)^{1/2}\n       \\eta\\bigl(y,x;\\delta(y,x)e^p\\bigr),\n       \\qquad\\eta\\in\\widetilde H.\n\\tag{LFT4.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe same substitution proves that the right side is square integrable with norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\eta\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\eta\\|\\)<\/span>. Apply the inverse Fourier unitary to obtain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>. This establishes surjectivity, with no unspecified completion or multiplicity space.<\/p>\n<h3>Compute on one orbit arrow, then on the whole algebra<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\pi(F)\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\pi(F)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde u_g\\)\">\\(\\widetilde u_g\\)<\/span> be the lifted generators of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j:M\\to N\\)\">\\(j:M\\to N\\)<\/span> be the lambda-independent field embedding from Section 3. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-REL.html#rel-5\">modular matrix formula<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (\\Pi_\\varphi(u_g)\\xi)(y,x;r)\n     =\\delta(y,g^{-1}y)^{-ir}\\xi(g^{-1}y,x;r).\n\\tag{LFT4.f}\n\\]\">\\[\n (\\Pi_\\varphi(u_g)\\xi)(y,x;r)\n     =\\delta(y,g^{-1}y)^{-ir}\\xi(g^{-1}y,x;r).\n\\tag{LFT4.f}\n\\]<\/span>\nPut <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=g^{-1}y\\)\">\\(z=g^{-1}y\\)<\/span>. Negative Fourier transformation turns the scalar multiplier in (LFT4.f) into the shift\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\mapsto p+\\log\\delta(y,z)\\)\">\\(p\\mapsto p+\\log\\delta(y,z)\\)<\/span>. But the cocycle identity gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)+\\log\\delta(y,z)\n        =\\log(\\delta(x,z)\\lambda).\n\\tag{LFT4.g}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\log(\\delta(x,y)\\lambda)+\\log\\delta(y,z)\n        =\\log(\\delta(x,z)\\lambda).\n\\tag{LFT4.g}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda^{-1/2}\\)\">\\(\\lambda^{-1/2}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is unchanged. Diagonal multipliers have no Fourier shift, since their original modular action is trivial. Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(\\pi(f))W^*&amp;=\\widetilde\\pi(f(y))=j(\\pi(f)),\\\\\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(u_g)W^*&amp;=\\widetilde u_g=j(u_g),\\\\\n W\\Lambda(t)W^*&amp;=\\widetilde\\pi(\\lambda_y^{-it}).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\tag{LFT4.h}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(\\pi(f))W^*&amp;=\\widetilde\\pi(f(y))=j(\\pi(f)),\\\\\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(u_g)W^*&amp;=\\widetilde u_g=j(u_g),\\\\\n W\\Lambda(t)W^*&amp;=\\widetilde\\pi(\\lambda_y^{-it}).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\tag{LFT4.h}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe notation in the last line means multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_y^{-it}\\)\">\\(\\lambda_y^{-it}\\)<\/span> on the represented lifted relation; its function on the lifted base is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((y,a)\\mapsto a^{-it}\\)\">\\((y,a)\\mapsto a^{-it}\\)<\/span>. The last equality follows because the Fourier transform of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi(r-t)\\)\">\\(\\xi(r-t)\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-itp}\\widehat\\xi(p)\\)\">\\(e^{-itp}\\widehat\\xi(p)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The translation and modulation identities hold on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span>, by the scalar theorem and tensor completion. Formula (LFT4.g) is a pointwise identity on the invariant conull relation. These facts justify (LFT4.h) as identities of bounded operators on their full Hilbert spaces. Moreover both maps\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\mapsto W\\Pi_\\varphi(A)W^*\\)\">\\(A\\mapsto W\\Pi_\\varphi(A)W^*\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\mapsto j(A)\\)\">\\(A\\mapsto j(A)\\)<\/span> are normal. They agree on the unital star algebra spanned by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(f)u_g\\)\">\\(\\pi(f)u_g\\)<\/span>, so ultraweak density and normality yield\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(A)W^*=j(A)\\qquad(A\\in M).\n\\tag{LFT4.i}\n\\]\">\\[\n W\\Pi_\\varphi(A)W^*=j(A)\\qquad(A\\in M).\n\\tag{LFT4.i}\n\\]<\/span>\nNo Fourier integral has been interchanged with an arbitrary infinite orbit-matrix sum.<\/p>\n<p>We also need the entire image algebra. The functions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\mapsto a^{-it}\\)\">\\(a\\mapsto a^{-it}\\)<\/span> generate every bounded Borel function of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in L\\)\">\\(a\\in L\\)<\/span>. One explicit verification uses only <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=1/n\\)\">\\(t=1/n\\)<\/span>: the continuous map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n r\\longmapsto(e^{-ir/n})_{n\\ge1}\\quad\n     \\text{from }\\mathbb R\\text{ to }\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N}\n\\tag{LFT4.j}\n\\]\">\\[\n r\\longmapsto(e^{-ir/n})_{n\\ge1}\\quad\n     \\text{from }\\mathbb R\\text{ to }\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N}\n\\tag{LFT4.j}\n\\]<\/span>\nis injective, since a nonzero difference cannot be an integer multiple of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\pi n\\)\">\\(2\\pi n\\)<\/span> for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. Its image is Borel and its inverse on that image is Borel by the <a href=\"../courses/NCG-FOLIATIONS/companions/polish-spaces-and-standard-borel-spaces.html#oa-fnd-pb-06\">Borel image and inverse theorem<\/a>. Spectral projections of the real and imaginary coordinate multipliers belong to their generated von Neumann algebra by the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf1.spectral-calculus\">bounded Borel spectral calculus<\/a>. The class of sets whose characteristic multipliers belong is closed under complements and countable disjoint unions, using bounded strong limits. A monotone-class argument therefore gives every Borel function of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, and hence every bounded Borel function of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=e^r\\)\">\\(a=e^r\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Together with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\pi(f(y))\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\pi(f(y))\\)<\/span>, these multipliers give every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\pi(F)\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\pi(F)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\in L^\\infty(X\\times L,\\mu\\,d\\lambda)\\)\">\\(F\\in L^\\infty(X\\times L,\\mu\\,d\\lambda)\\)<\/span>. To see the joint assertion, rectangle indicators belong; their finite intersections are again rectangles. Complements and disjoint bounded strong sums give a lambda-system, so the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L75.html#oa-flow.kernel.integration\">proved pi–lambda argument<\/a> gives all product Borel indicators. Simple approximation gives bounded functions, and completion only changes null representatives. The representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\pi\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\pi\\)<\/span> is normal and faithful by REL2 applied to the lifted system. Thus (LFT4.h) contains all lifted diagonal generators and all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde u_g\\)\">\\(\\widetilde u_g\\)<\/span>. The reverse inclusion of generating algebras is already in (LFT4.h)–(LFT4.i), proving the spatial normal isomorphism\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Gamma:C_\\varphi\\longrightarrow N,\\qquad\n \\Gamma(B)=WBW^*,\\qquad \\Gamma(C_\\varphi)=N.\n\\tag{LFT4.k}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Gamma:C_\\varphi\\longrightarrow N,\\qquad\n \\Gamma(B)=WBW^*,\\qquad \\Gamma(C_\\varphi)=N.\n\\tag{LFT4.k}\n\\]<\/span>\nIts inverse is conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W^*\\)\">\\(W^*\\)<\/span>, also normal.<\/p>\n<h3>The dual flow and the full density domain<\/h3>\n<p>Fourier modulation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-isr}\\)\">\\(e^{-isr}\\)<\/span> shifts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p+s\\)\">\\(p+s\\)<\/span>. In (LFT4.c) this replaces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^s\\lambda\\)\">\\(e^s\\lambda\\)<\/span>. Its square-root factor gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n W(e^{-isr}\\xi)(y,x;\\lambda)\n    =e^{s/2}(W\\xi)(y,x;e^s\\lambda).\n\\tag{LFT4.l}\n\\]\">\\[\n W(e^{-isr}\\xi)(y,x;\\lambda)\n    =e^{s/2}(W\\xi)(y,x;e^s\\lambda).\n\\tag{LFT4.l}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe right side is the dilation unitary implementing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s\\)\">\\(\\theta_s\\)<\/span> in Section 3. Therefore\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\widehat\\theta_s=\\theta_s\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\widehat\\theta_s=\\theta_s\\Gamma\\)<\/span> on the whole algebra. The core's positive nonsingular generator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_\\varphi\\)\">\\(h_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, characterized by\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_\\varphi^{it}=\\Lambda(t)\\)\">\\(h_\\varphi^{it}=\\Lambda(t)\\)<\/span>, is constructed with its full spectral domains in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-1\">CORE1<\/a>. The last line of (LFT4.h), or uniqueness of that self-adjoint generator, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Gamma(h_\\varphi)=M_{\\lambda_y^{-1}},\\qquad\n D\\bigl(\\Gamma(h_\\varphi)^z\\bigr)\n   =\\left\\{\\eta\\in\\widetilde H:\n       \\int_{E\\times L}\\lambda_y^{-2\\operatorname{Re}z}\n                    |\\eta|^2\\,d\\nu_r\\,d\\lambda&lt;\\infty\\right\\}.\n\\tag{LFT4.m}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Gamma(h_\\varphi)=M_{\\lambda_y^{-1}},\\qquad\n D\\bigl(\\Gamma(h_\\varphi)^z\\bigr)\n   =\\left\\{\\eta\\in\\widetilde H:\n       \\int_{E\\times L}\\lambda_y^{-2\\operatorname{Re}z}\n                    |\\eta|^2\\,d\\nu_r\\,d\\lambda&lt;\\infty\\right\\}.\n\\tag{LFT4.m}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>, and the operator value is multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_y^{-z}\\)\">\\(\\lambda_y^{-z}\\)<\/span> on this domain. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(h_\\varphi)=e^{-s}h_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(h_\\varphi)=e^{-s}h_\\varphi\\)<\/span> in these coordinates. The reciprocal height, not the height, is forced by the chosen translation and Fourier signs.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-LFT.md","start_offset":21043,"end_offset":28896,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
